{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310843,"nonce":"j8xI8trmH0XR1HIFhzfnb6fP9/g=","title":"Llama fused-PREFILL CoreML graph engine (next-lever #1) DONE + coherent. nue.engine.PrefillGraph runs the whole 16-block stack in ONE ...","message":"**What:** `nue.engine.PrefillGraph` (Haxe extern) + `rayzor-tensors/src/prefill_graph.rs`\n(handle registry) + `coreml_shim.m::rzt_coreml_predict_prefill`. Authored by\n`rayzor-tensors/examples/llama_prefill_author.py`: full 16-block stack (RMSNorm, INTERLEAVED\nrope matching `rayzor_tensor_rope`, GQA, causal mask, SwiGLU), Q4_K→fp16 baked, per-bucket\nfixed-shape `\u003cstem\u003e.prefill_s{S}.mlmodelc` (S∈{128,512}) next to the gguf. numpy-validated\nhidden cos 0.9994, KV 0.999+. One `execute` writes final hidden + `[layers][2][bucket*kvh*hd]`\nKV; `LlamaModel.graphPrefill` copies each slot's real-prompt prefix into `Tensor.uninit` buffers\nand `cache.append`s them, so CPU decode continues from the graph's KV (UMA zero-copy). Coherent:\n\"Gravity is a fundamental force of nature that attracts two objects with…\". Auto-detected at load\nin `GGUFLoader.applyEngines` (both load paths); `NUE_PREFILL=off` kills.\n\n**ttft reality (the \"why 5s\" answer):** NOT copies/vectorization (kvfill=6ms). It's TWO\ncold-start costs: (a) CoreML E5RT graph specialization ~3.5s COLD, disk-caches to ~1.6s, and\nin-process WARM (2nd+ predict) 0.15s; (b) first-call JIT/resolve of the graphPrefill call tree\n~1.6s. Baseline per-op prefill = 1.33-1.64s ttft. So graph prefill is a **WARM/SERVER win**\n(96-150ms/prefill after warmup) and a **cold one-shot-CLI wash/regression**. Decode itself is\nfast (~90-110 tok/s, 8 tok in ~0.07-0.25s). `LlamaModel.warmPrefill()` (opt-in\n`NUE_PREFILL_WARM=1`) does one dummy forward at load to pay both cold costs once — for servers.\nDo NOT sell graph prefill as a cold-CLI ttft win. See [[feedback_gpu_not_a_cpu_lever]] context:\nCPU-only (ANE rejects llama dims — ANECCompile fails).\n\n**Architecture rule (user-enforced, several interjections):** CoreML graph ENGINES are nue\nfeatures end-to-end, NOT stdlib. Final layout after the full move:\n- Haxe: `nue.engine.{PrefillGraph,BertGraph}` (moved OUT of `rayzor.ds`). NEVER an ML method on\n  stdlib `rayzor.ds.Tensor`.\n- Rust: `nue-plugins/src/{prefill_graph,bert_graph,coreml_shim.m}` — MOVED from rayzor-tensors.\n  nue-plugins/build.rs now compiles the ObjC shim + links CoreML/Foundation (cc build-dep,\n  macos-gated); rayzor-tensors keeps ONLY Accelerate (apple_accel AMX/BNNS). Symbols renamed\n  `rayzor_*_graph_*`→`nue_*_graph_*` and internal shim `rzt_coreml_*`→`nue_coreml_*`; registered\n  in nue-plugins `plugin_init` (non-wasm gated — std + CoreML). rayzor-tensors is pure tensor\n  kernels again. BONUS: the `.rzb` bundle path loads ONLY libnue_plugins.dylib, so graph-prefill\n  now works in bundles too. The graph modules DO declare an `extern \"C\"` block for the in-dylib\n  `nue_coreml_*` shim (intra-module, not a host reach) — the one exception to nue-plugins'\n  \"no host extern blocks\" rule.\n\n1. **Extern return-type DECAY (FIXED, compilation.rs:~7615).** Bodyless `@:native` extern methods\n   were registered with `return_type: 3 // PtrVoid` hardcoded → a scalar return (`:Int`/`:Bool`/\n   `:Float`) decays to a boxed pointer → **null in string-concat**, garbage as a Tensor handle.\n   (The raw i64 was actually fine for compares/arith — `rc != 0`, `handle \u003e 0`, `bucket` all\n   worked; only `\"x\" + externInt()` showed \"null\".) Fix: new `haxe_type_to_native_tag` maps the\n   real declared `method.return_type` into the native-tag scheme consumed by\n   `compiler_plugin::native_type_to_descriptor` (0=Void 1=I64 2=F64 3=PtrVoid 4=Bool — DIFFERENT\n   numbering than `haxe_type_to_descriptor`, which is 3=I32/9=Ptr; do NOT confuse them). Params\n   left PtrVoid (they ride the `external_function_param_types` carrier). Validated llama + BERT.\n   Return-carrier gap confirmed by Explore map: there is NO `external_function_return_types` map;\n   the func_id path (hir_to_mir.rs:17897) already recovers return type from\n   `extern_functions[id].signature.return_type` but is skipped when `get_function_id` drifts\n   x-module; the @:native path (17848) uses `get_extern_function_signature`→the hardcoded desc.\n2. **Same-name collapse FIXED IN COMPILER (a17124a3) — NOT by the relocation (I was wrong).**\n   `PrefillGraph.bucketFor/execute` (arity 2/5) collapse onto `BertGraph`'s identical name+arity;\n   `load` (arity 8 ≠ bert's 6) does NOT, so it resolved and printed \"engine=graph on\" — masking\n   that bucketFor returned 0 (bert's empty registry) → graph DISABLED → per-op fallthrough. I\n   first claimed the nue.engine relocation fixed this; it did NOT — I mis-validated on coherent\n   output (per-op is also coherent). Real repro: `bucketFor` returns 0 vs 128. ROOT: classes are\n   registered DOT-qualified (`nue.engine.PrefillGraph`) but `runtime_mapping::class_matches` (408)\n   only did exact / `_`-suffix, so a bare `PrefillGraph` lookup missed → fell to a name-only path\n   that grabbed the first same-name candidate (BertGraph). FIX = add DOT-suffix to class_matches\n   (`registered.ends_with(\".{lookup}\")`), the package-qualified analog of the `_` rule. Natural\n   names (load/bucketFor/execute/kvCopy) now resolve correctly; BERT unaffected. LESSON: coherent\n   text ≠ graph ran — always assert bucketFor\u003e0 / execS\u003e0, not just output quality.\n\nWith the graph actually running + `warmPrefill` (NUE_PREFILL_WARM=1, one forward at load pays the\nCoreML E5RT specialization once): **llama prefill ttft = 0.16-0.18s**, warm graph predict\nexecS=0.10-0.17s, decode ~42 tok/s. vs per-op baseline 1.3-1.6s. All prior \"warm 1.5-1.8s\" numbers\nwere PER-OP (graph was disabled by the collapse). Cold (no warmup) still pays ~3.9s specialization\non the first predict → graph is a warm/server win; warmup opt-in. OPEN POLICY: graph auto-enables\nwhen artifacts present (opt-out NUE_PREFILL=off), but WITHOUT warmup cold ttft (3.9s) \u003e per-op\n(1.5s) — a one-shot-CLI regression. Decide default: gate graph on warm-context, or auto-warm, or\nkeep graph opt-in. NOT batchable over prompts yet (fixed (S,HIDDEN), no B dim) — a throughput\nlever, orthogonal to ttft.\n\nChasing \"server decode fell from steady 85-90 to variable 54-83\". Findings + fixes:\n- **Phase separation (verified in code):** AMX (`RZT_AMX_PREFILL`, quant.rs) is gated `m \u003e= RZT_AMX_MIN_BATCH` (default **16**); CoreML graph is prefill-only. So **decode (m=1) always = SDOT NEON int8 dot**, skipping BOTH. AMX/CoreML are TTFT/prefill levers ONLY. The requant lm_head (Q6_K→Q4_K_M, LlamaArch.hx:151 default-ON opt-out) is a DECODE op (batch-1 last-row/per-token) → SDOT; that's its win. `NUE_PREFILL=off` is the ONLY graph kill — turning off AMX+warm leaves the graph ON (cold) overriding morsels.\n- **Streaming was the bottleneck (not decode kernel):** server `STREAM=1` did per-token `out.flush()` (TCP syscall) ON the decode thread → 54-83 vs STREAM=0's 88-92. CLI had async writer (46806fd7); server never adopted it. FIX (24121d42): writer thread owns the socket + producer coalescing (per-token=array push, channel per 8-token batch) → STREAM=1 tracks STREAM=0 (~86 median). REQUIRED the compiler derive fix (socket wasn't Send).\n- **Compiler: extern derive metadata not persisted cross-file (e13c6304).** SendSyncValidator sees only `typed_file.classes` (per-file), so `@:derive([Send])` on stdlib `sys.net.SocketOutput` was invisible at a `Thread.spawn` capture site. FIX: mirror Send/Sync derives onto the SYMBOL (`SymbolFlags::DERIVE_SEND/SYNC` 1\u003c\u003c23/24, global) at class lowering; trait_checker falls back to symbol flags when class not in local list. OPEN follow-ups (user-directed): (1) derives on INTERFACES; (2) analysis-phase check that field types fulfill the derived trait.\n- **Decode warmup, decoupled from graph (04162bd1).** Bundle tier-promotes by call count (`WARM=30`), so the FIRST 30-token request's decode runs pre-promotion (low-floor ~42 tok/s). warmPrefill→`warm()`, graph-gate dropped, warms BOTH forwardIds+forwardLastLogits (40 single-token forwards each) at load. Gate `NUE_DECODE_WARM` (default ON, opt-out), moved OUT of the graph block so pure-CPU (`NUE_PREFILL=off`) warms too. Pure-CPU median 54→81; req1 42→66. Residual: req1 (66) \u003c steady (92) = micro-arch warming 40 iters don't fully cover (real prompt seeds bigger cache); more iters would help.\n- **Bench hygiene:** `bench_server` shows CLIENT output only (server `[warm]`/`[load]` go to its own log). Machine load (my builds + XProtect/mds scanning fresh binaries + GB artifacts) was a real, repeatedly-measured confound (load 10+) that muddied run-to-run variance — but the code causes above were real and reproducible.\n\nfmt+clippy clean (both crates + examples); llama + BERT validated after the full move. On `main`\n(branch before commit). Changes: `compiler/src/compilation.rs` (Bug#3); MOVED\n`{bert_graph.rs,prefill_graph.rs,coreml_shim.m}` rayzor-tensors→nue-plugins/src/ + moved examples\n`{bert_engine_smoke,prefill_engine_smoke}.rs` + python authors; nue-plugins {lib.rs,build.rs,\nCargo.toml} gain the engines+CoreML; rayzor-tensors {lib.rs,build.rs,tensor.rs} shed them (keep\nAccelerate); NEW `nue/nue/engine/{PrefillGraph,BertGraph}.hx`; `LlamaModel.hx` (graphPrefill +\nwarmPrefill), `{BertEmbedder,BertModel,GGUFLoader}.hx` (imports+wiring); deleted\n`rayzor.ds/{BertGraph,PrefillGraph}.hx`. Next levers: batched encode, param-retention bisect,\nQwen2/ViT.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_llama_prefill_graph` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}